{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2609.03904","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2609.03904","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2609.03904","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Extremal Families for Matchings in Permutations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05D05, 05A05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-09-03T14:23:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-04T00:58:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Two permutations $σ,τ\\in S_n$ are called disjoint if the composition $στ^{-1}$ has no fixed point. If a family $\\mathcal F\\subseteq S_n$ contains no $s$ pairwise disjoint permutations, then a simple averaging argument gives $|\\mathcal F|\\leq(s-1)(n-1)!$. Inozemtsev, Kolupaev and Kupavskii characterized the equality cases in the range $s\\leq n/(2^{17}\\log n).$ We characterize all equality cases throughout the range $2\\le s\\le n$: equality holds if and only if $\\mathcal F$ is a union of $(s-1)$ pairwise disjoint $1$-cosets. We also prove the linear statement underlying this classification: a real-valued function on $S_n$ has constant sum on every one-factorization if and only if it lies in the span of the indicators of the $1$-cosets. The proof is combinatorial and applies to every order, with a few small orders handled separately."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.03904","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-09-04T02:24:58Z","registered":"2026-09-04T02:24:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-04T02:24:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.15873","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.15873","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.15873","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murali","familyName":"Menon","name":"Menon, Murali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Two Questions on $G$-harmonic Tuples"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Group Theory (math.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"20D60, 20D05, 20B30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-16T17:49:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:02:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"An $n$-tuple of positive integers is $G$-harmonic if there are subgroups of $G$ having those indices whose cosets can be chosen pairwise disjoint, and $\\mathbb{Z}$-harmonic if there are pairwise disjoint residue classes with those moduli. Ginosar asked whether every $G$-harmonic tuple is $\\mathbb{Z}$-harmonic. Margolis and Schnabel proved this for tuples of length at most $4$, and analysed a particular family of length-$5$ tuples that would yield a counterexample if any member were $G$-harmonic. We show that the bound $4$ is sharp: $(6,6,6,10,15)$ is $A_5$-harmonic but not $\\mathbb{Z}$-harmonic. Moreover, the five pairwise disjoint cosets realising this tuple can be extended to a coset partition of $A_5$ using only cosets of indices $6$, $10$, and $15$. The index tuple of this partition is not $\\mathbb{Z}$-harmonic; because its indices repeat, this does not contradict the Herzog--Schönheim conjecture. We also prove that no member of the length-$5$ family analysed by Margolis and Schnabel in connection with possible counterexamples is $G$-harmonic for any group $G$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.15873","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-18T03:26:35Z","registered":"2026-08-18T03:26:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:26:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2309.04066","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2309.04066","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2309.04066","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elizabeth","familyName":"Athaide","name":"Athaide, Elizabeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Cardwell","name":"Cardwell, Emma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christina","familyName":"Thompson","name":"Thompson, Christina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Class Number Formulas for Certain Biquadratic Fields"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2023,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11R16, 11R29, 11A63","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2023-09-08T01:47:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2023-09-11T00:08:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2023-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2023","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider the class numbers of imaginary quadratic extensions $F(\\sqrt{-p})$, for certain primes $p$, of totally real quadratic fields $F$ which have class number one. Using seminal work of Shintani, we obtain two elementary class number formulas for many such fields. The first expresses the class number as an alternating sum of terms that we generate from the coefficients of the power series expansions of two simple rational functions that depend on the arithmetic of $F$ and $p$. The second makes use of expansions of $1/p$, where $p$ is a prime such that $p \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$ and $p$ remains inert in $F$. More precisely, for a generator $\\varepsilon_F$ of the totally positive unit group of $\\mathcal{O}_F$, the base-$\\varepsilon_{F}$ expansion of $1/p$ has period length $\\ell_{F,p}$, and our second class number formula expresses the class number as a finite sum over disjoint cosets of size $\\ell_{F,p}$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27pages, 2tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.04066","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2023-09-11T01:07:51Z","registered":"2023-09-11T01:07:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2023-09-11T01:07:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.0807.2207","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.0807.2207","identifiers":[{"identifier":"0807.2207","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wan-Jie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Wan-Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Sun's conjecture concerning disjoint cosets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2008,"subjects":[{"subject":"Group Theory (math.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"20D60; 05E99; 11B75; 20F99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2008-07-14T16:58:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2009-12-01T11:02:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2008-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2008","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"ScholarlyArticle","resourceTypeGeneral":"Text","citeproc":"article-journal","bibtex":"article","ris":"RPRT","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In 2004, Zhi-Wei Sun posed the following conjecture: If a_1G_1,...,a_kG_k (k\u0026gt;1) are finitely many pairwise disjoint left cosets in a group G with all the indices [G:G_i] finite, then for some 1\\le i"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.2207","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2022-03-16T06:39:02Z","registered":"2022-03-16T06:39:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2022-03-16T06:39:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2107.07833","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2107.07833","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2107.07833","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuval","familyName":"Filmus","name":"Filmus, Yuval","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Boolean functions on $S_n$ which are nearly linear"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2021,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2021-07-16T11:42:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2021-07-19T00:14:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2021-12-10T11:25:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2021-12-13T01:14:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2021-07","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2021","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"ScholarlyArticle","resourceTypeGeneral":"Text","citeproc":"article-journal","bibtex":"article","ris":"RPRT","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.19086/da.30186","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that if $f\\colon S_n \\to \\{0,1\\}$ is $ε$-close to linear in $L_2$ and $\\mathbb{E}[f] \\leq 1/2$ then $f$ is $O(ε)$-close to a union of \"mostly disjoint\" cosets, and moreover this is sharp: any such union is close to linear. This constitutes a sharp Friedgut-Kalai-Naor theorem for the symmetric group. Using similar techniques, we show that if $f\\colon S_n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is linear, $\\Pr[f \\notin \\{0,1\\}] \\leq ε$, and $\\Pr[f = 1] \\leq 1/2$, then $f$ is $O(ε)$-close to a union of mostly disjoint cosets, and this is also sharp; and that if $f\\colon S_n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is linear and $ε$-close to $\\{0,1\\}$ in $L_\\infty$ then $f$ is $O(ε)$-close in $L_\\infty$ to a union of disjoint cosets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07833","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2022-02-21T21:23:11Z","registered":"2022-02-21T21:23:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2022-02-21T21:23:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":5,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=%22disjoint+cosets%22\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=50"}}